WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.920
I think we have a lot
of preconceptions

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.480
about what race really is.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.280
What is race has changed
dramatically through time.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.880
There is no official scientific
definition of race.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.280
We're all different.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.320
I was aware that I wasn't white.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.520
The teacher said,
"No, no, Tess is something else."

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.600
And everybody's eyes are on me,
and I'm a really shy person.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.720
She said, "But what are you, Tessa?"

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.400
A lot of people imagine that there is
some biological tangibility to race,

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.560
because we use it so much.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.760
Race really is a social construct,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.680
but just because something
is a social construct,

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.320
that doesn't mean it doesn't have
meaning in people's lives

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.040
like money, or the nation state,
or democracy,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.920
or all these other things
that define how we live,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.880
but are also constructed.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.360
It influences what you're eating,

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.760
it influences the type of healthcare

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:01.720
you have access to,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.360
the type of schooling
you have access to,

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.640
how people feel about you.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.560
I mean, it's a powerful marker
for all of these things

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.080
that we care about when
we're trying to run a society.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.720
Race has never been something
that I don't have to think about.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.800
It's put on to us.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.800
When you look at the history
and origins of this idea,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.440
you start to see it
for what it really is,

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.400
which is the manifestation of power.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.040
People imagine that people have
always thought about skin colour

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.480
in this racialised way,
and we really haven't.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.320
That idea is no more than
a few hundred years old.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.000
It's not really until the age
of colonialism, in the 17th Century,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.880
that you start getting the idea that

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.200
each continent has its own
individual kind of people.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.920
It arose at a very specific
moment in history.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.400
In the 17th Century,
you're taking long trips by ship,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.160
and you're struck by
how different people look

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.400
than you remember them
when you got on.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.600
So this is the first edition
of Systema Naturae,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.160
so the Systems of Nature.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.600
And for man, Linnaeus distinguishes
four varieties,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.800
these varieties correspond
to the four continents.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.120
Where it really changes is in the
10th edition of Systema Naturae.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.720
This is the foundation
of scientific racism.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.120
He brings in moral character,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.560
so the American
is "red and choleric,"

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.160
and stands up straight.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.720
The European, "white, sanguine,
and muscular"

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.240
The Asian, "sallow, melancholic
and stiff"

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.760
The African, "black,
phlegmatic and lazy"

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.720
So these stereotypes have endured
through the ages.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.400
If you're going to take
a group of people,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.920
and you're going to decide that
they're not people anymore,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.840
then you need something
to justify that.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.640
You can be defined very differently

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.160
depending on the country
that you're in,

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.560
or the time that you're in.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.280
And that's how these categories
have always been used.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.360
They are political tools

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.200
that change depending
on their usefulness

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.000
to whoever is doing
the categorisation.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.600
We are one of the most homogeneous
species on the planet.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.640
Humans have far less genetic
variation than chimpanzees do.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.240
And yet, those chimpanzees look
the same to me.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.080
There were never any "pure" races,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.600
there is no gene that exists
in all the members of one race

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:10.800
and not another.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.000
There is no black gene,
there is no white gene.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.680
Human groups overlap completely
in their genetic variation.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.760
In the same way that you're related
to your family,

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.520
you can get a kind of
fuzzy genetic similarity.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.200
But when you get to
the continental level,

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.160
which is really where
we're talking about race,

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.680
that genetic similarity is so fuzzy,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.520
and so statistically weak,
as to be almost meaningless.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.840
We have this envy of fields where
things are hyper-simplistic.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.160
We want to be able to look
at a genome and say

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.520
that people are these kind
of concrete immutable things,

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:47.760
that cannot be changed.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.480
Genetics doesn't work that way,
it's the product of many genes

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.960
interacting with each other
and their environments.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.680
To fully answer the question about
what a living thing is

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.920
it's going to be about much more
than genes.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.640
If I were to go out
on to the street today

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.600
and find someone
of Indian heritage like myself,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.360
and randomly then pick someone
who is not of Indian heritage,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.120
it is perfectly
statistically possible

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.440
for my genome to have more
in common with that person

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.440
who isn't of Indian heritage
than that person who is.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.560
Even though race itself
is a construct,

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.960
racism is real and we are not
in a post-racial society.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.280
Race keeps making itself
and power keeps making race.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.480
Even though we know that
race isn't biological,

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.160
we've been living with this idea
of it being real

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.520
for so long that it means that people
who do have a shared racial identity

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.200
have now cultural similarities

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.320
which has been born out of hardship,

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.320
but it's still beautiful
and important.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.440
The biology of race
is not a useful concept.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.960
I have a different
historical trajectory,

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.000
I love the African
American tradition,

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.640
but it's not better than
anybody else's,

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.080
it's just the one that I'm a part of.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.200
I don't think we're going
to stop classifying people,

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.960
because I think that's how
we make sense of the world.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.680
I think every generation
is going to classify people

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:20.600
according to the criteria that
are politically important to them.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.080
I don't think we need to find
new boxes to put people in,

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.560
I think we now have
the opportunity to say,

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.120
we understand that all of these
things are a spectrum,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.960
in a way that we hadn't
considered before.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.520
So instead of finding new ways
to categorise people

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.600
we can just find new ways
to exist better together.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.600
Race is a story that's handed down
and handed around.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.400
But we can choose
the stories that we tell.

